; A `.cbor` target reached through a MULTI-LEVEL alias chain generates, in every position the
; single-level siblings cover (tests/robustness/choice_cbor_ref_arm.cddl,
; tests/robustness/cbor_ref_rule_body.cddl) — this row is the DEPTH axis those two do not have.
;
; Both of those fixtures pin a chain of length one (`bar = uint`), and DEPTH is not a variable at
; any of the three seams, because of an invariant held elsewhere: `register_type_alias` refuses an
; already-`Alias`-wrapped base, so the alias table never stores a nested alias, so
; `resolve_alias`/`new_type` never hand back more than one level. Each link flattens as it
; registers — `parsing::strip_alias_for_registration` is the single site that does it, and it
; carries the link's wire facts across the strip so a depth-N chain answers the emitter's lookup at
; every position a depth-1 chain does. A `.cbor` target therefore arrives here as one `Alias` level
; whatever the chain behind it, and a four-link chain and a one-link chain are the identical
; `RustType`. Without this row, that invariant is load-bearing for a shape nothing exercises, and a
; future change that lets a nested alias into the table would only surface at depth.
;
; The rule NAMES are deliberately sorted so the chain links do NOT sort before their users
; (`aaa_a` uses `bbb_b` uses `mmm_c` uses `zzz_d`): the rule-graph edge, not name order, is what has
; to order them, and a name-order coincidence is exactly how the rule-body sibling used to generate
; or abort by accident.
zzz_d = uint
mmm_c = zzz_d
bbb_b = mmm_c
aaa_a = bbb_b
arm = bytes .cbor aaa_a / tstr
memb = [f: bytes .cbor aaa_a]
body = bytes .cbor aaa_a
